---- Compilation ----
You will need openwatcom and python3.
Instructions are for Linux-based operating systems.
The following need to be set in the working terminal for an x86_64 machine:

export WATCOM=/opt/watcom
export PATH="$WATCOM/binl64:$PATH"
export EDPATH="$WATCOM/eddat"
export WIPFC="$WATCOM/wipfc"
export INCLUDE="$WATCOM/h"

Then, just run 'cm' to compile.

This moves all assets and compiled data into release/ where you can run
    AUTOEXEC.BAT in a pc98 environment to initialise everything necessary.

---- How to add assets ----
Adding assets using the provided conversion tools is easy.

-- Images --
Before doing anything else, it is recommended to crop, scale, or initially
    design every image to be 4:3 aspect ratio, 640x400 pixels, with 16 colours.
    This means that the converter won't make assumptions that could make the
    images look strange. It can by all means convert other images, it is just
    better if you do it beforehand so you can iron out any artefacts.

To convert images, simply use `./convert.bg [image folder]` and it'll tell you
    if any images didn't get converted.

Since the colours of everything on screen is defined in the palette, make sure
    that the images you use have enough contrast between the brightest and
    darkest points, otherwise you won't be able to see anything. The way around
    this is that if your image is too dark (maybe a dungeon), add a single
    pixel of pure white and a single pixel of grey at the bottom (so it cannot
    be seen). This means the colour palette will contain enough colours of
    sufficient contrast.

-- Fonts --
The provided font is the Toshiba Satellite Bios font, but if this font doesn't
    fit your project, you can provide your own. Make sure the font is an 8px
    font in TTF format.

To convert a font, simply use `./convert.font [font name]` and it shall convert
    it to the required format.

-- Story --
The master story file is located at ASSETS/STORY.P98
The engine reads the first character of each line to choose what to do and
    these are the options:

` comment
/ new speaker
;NUM set typewriter delay to NUM milliseconds
; turn typewriter mode off
$ dialogue (wait for keypress)
$$ dialogue (non-waiting)
# newline (within dialogue statement)
@ label definition
: jump to label
? user menu branch (action1|label1&action2|label2)
% show image
_ wait for keypress
~ play music
~! stop music
* load new story file
** go back to prev story file (only 1 story deep)
*@FILE:NUM load any story file at a line number
! end branch/game

Note that any arguments such as those given to % or * do not require file
    extensions. Just provide the filename and it will load it.

Once a statement is issued, that state will persist until a counter-statement
    is issued. For example, % will display an image until another % is
    encountered.

If a ! is not found at the end of a branch, it will just carry through to the
    next processable statement.

Dialogue can be up to 6 lines on one page.

A single line in the story file can be up to 256 characters long. The maximum
    amount of dialogue able to be put on a single screen is around 200
    characters.

- Example -

%SUN
/Alice
$ Hello there!
/Bob
;20
$$ I want a beer.
;
%TREES
?Go to the pub|PUB&Drink at home|HOME

@PUB
%PUB
$ You have a good time at the pub
!

@HOME
%HOME
$ You die of loneliness. Don't drink alone.
!


This example shows the image SUN, makes Alice say  "Hello there!", waits for
    the user, makes Bob say "I want a beer" with a typewriter effect with delay
    of 20ms, immediately shows the image TREES, then asks the user to go to the
    pub or drink at home. If they choose to drink at the pub, they are shown
    the picture of the pub and have a good time. The story ends there. If they
    chose to drink at home, they die and then the story ends.
